This issue is the canonical contract for a new `@ariakit/ui` layout component, the **Shell**, designed over four feedback rounds (r1 to r4, 2026-09-14). All 25 design decisions are settled. The interactive design document with prototypes, experiments and research reports stays at https://claude.ai/code/artifact/1b435401-4f68-46e9-aadc-1e519a3ad584 (maintainer-only; every measurement this contract depends on is restated below, so this issue stands on its own) for examples and future feedback; from here on, contract changes are made in this issue.

Scope of this issue: the public contract and the implementation obligations. No implementation exists yet; `packages/ariakit-ui/src` has no shell or sidebar code (the previous `Sidebar` component was removed in #7460).

![Shell playground prototype: sticky blurred header, start navigation sidebar, centered main with a full-bleed band, end table of contents; the readout shows the content column on the shell's center](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fbaff265-ed9d-441a-8009-97fd37adf47c)

## Summary

`Shell` assembles a header, up to two sidebars per side, a main area and a footer from optional parts, in one CSS grid, with no measurement, no `position: fixed` and no JavaScript breakpoint. The root declares the geometry once (sidebar widths, header height); every part reads it, and the main area uses it to keep its content column on the shell's center whatever the sidebars are doing. Sidebars fold with a drawer motion on wide shells and become modal Ariakit Dialogs on narrow ones, from one element and one state. Everything renders as static HTML and CSS; modality on narrow shells is the one behavior that needs JavaScript.

## Goals

- Assemble an app or docs page from optional parts, in one place, without hand-written offset math.
- Keep a top bar's middle part centered on the page while both ends fit, move it over when one end needs the room, and say which part gives way when nothing fits.
- Show and hide side panels with a drawer motion on desktop, and as a modal drawer on phones, from one component and one state.
- Place a panel under the header (docs) or beside it at full height (apps), on either side, and combine several panels, including two on one side.
- Center the main content on the page whatever the sidebars are doing, and let content react to the width it actually has.

### Supported use cases

| Use case | Parts | What must hold |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Docs site (Ariakit's own) | Sticky blurred header, sticky nav sidebar under it, centered main, table of contents at the end, footer | Anchors land under the header. The nav scrolls on its own. Content stays centered on the page even though the nav and the table of contents differ in width. |
| Dashboard | Full-height nav sidebar beside a header, optional detail panel at the end | Main content reacts to the width it has, not the window. The sidebar folds to a modal drawer on narrow shells. |
| Chat app | Rail plus channel list on the start, main with its own composer, member list at the end | Two sidebars on one side. The shell is locked to the viewport with consumer utilities. Nothing is measured. |
| Settings page | Header, inner sidebar with section links, centered main | Same shell, fewer parts. |
| Marketing page | Header and footer only | Zero sidebars must not leave gaps or empty tracks. |
| Phone | Any of the above under 48rem | Sidebars become modal drawers with a backdrop; the header's center part truncates, shifts or stacks; the footer stays a static bar. A bottom bar that must stay in view is a locked-shell layout. |

### Non-goals for v1

Resizable sidebars, hide-on-scroll headers, split panes, docked tab panels and swipe gestures (D19); the widths are root tokens, so a consumer can still drive resizing from their own handle without a recipe change. A main-scrolling "locked" shell is not a variant (D13); it is a documented consumer recipe. A collapsed icon rail is a follow-up designed with `Nav` (D17). Standalone bar, drawer and centered-column recipes are a possible later split, not part of v1 (D20). Sticky footers do not exist (D25).

### Compatibility promises

- Recipes render correctly as static HTML and CSS with no JavaScript (Astro pages, server rendering, Solid later). Modality on narrow shells needs JavaScript; the CSS overlay is its pre-hydration baseline.
- All positioning uses logical properties, so RTL pages mirror without extra rules.
- No `position: fixed` in the recipes, so nothing depends on which ancestors create a containing block.
- No breakpoint in JavaScript: the container query decides, and the component reads the outcome.
- The shell root, the main area and each sidebar are named query containers: `shell`, `shell-main`, `shell-sidebar` (the bars are `shell-header` and `shell-footer`).
- New public surface, plus one addition to an existing recipe: a `$bleed` variant on `Frame` (`packages/ariakit-ui/src/styles/frame.ts`) that places the frame on main's `full` grid lines. No existing behavior changes.

## Structure

The root is one CSS grid. Five columns: two sidebar tracks on each side of a flexible main track. Three rows: header, main, footer. Empty `auto` tracks are zero wide, so a shell with two parts costs nothing for the parts it does not have. The four sidebar tracks are the shell's slots: a sidebar takes one by its side and by its order among same-side sidebars in the DOM, and the root declares one width per slot.

```css
.shell {
  --shell-sidebar-width: 16rem;                       /* $sidebarWidth: default for every slot */
  --shell-start-1-width: var(--shell-sidebar-width);  /* $startWidth: outermost start slot */
  --shell-start-2-width: var(--shell-sidebar-width);  /*             inner start slot */
  --shell-end-1-width: var(--shell-sidebar-width);    /* $endWidth:  inner end slot (next to main) */
  --shell-end-2-width: var(--shell-sidebar-width);    /*             outermost end slot */
  --shell-header-height: 3.25rem;                     /* $headerHeight */
  --shell-duration: 300ms;                            /* $duration */
  --shell-motion: 1;                                  /* 0 under prefers-reduced-motion */
  --shell-time: calc(var(--shell-duration) * var(--shell-motion));
  --shell-ease: cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1);
  --shell-main-max-width: 48rem;
  --shell-gutter: 1.5rem;
  --shell-header-offset: 0px;                         /* derived: header height when the header is sticky */
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    [shell-start] auto auto
    [main-start] minmax(0, 1fr) [main-end]
    auto auto [shell-end];
  grid-template-rows:
    [shell-start header-start] auto
    [header-end main-start] minmax(0, 1fr)
    [main-end footer-start] auto
    [footer-end shell-end];
  min-block-size: 100cqb;      /* falls back to the small viewport without a size container */
  container: shell / inline-size;
  overflow-x: clip;            /* the drawer slides out of view without a scrollbar */
  position: relative;          /* the sidebar backdrop is positioned against the root */
  isolation: isolate;          /* chrome stacks below Ariakit overlays (z-index 10) */
}
.shell:has(> .shell-header[data-sticky]) { --shell-header-offset: var(--shell-header-height); }
.shell-header { grid-column: shell-start / shell-end; grid-row: header-start / header-end; }
.shell-footer { grid-column: shell-start / shell-end; grid-row: footer-start / footer-end; }
.shell-main   { grid-column: main-start / main-end;   grid-row: main-start / main-end; }
.shell-sidebar { grid-row: main-start / main-end; }
.shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] { grid-column: 1; --shell-slot-width: var(--shell-start-1-width); }
.shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] ~ .shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] { grid-column: 2; --shell-slot-width: var(--shell-start-2-width); }
.shell-sidebar[data-side="end"] { grid-column: 4; --shell-slot-width: var(--shell-end-1-width); }
.shell-sidebar[data-side="end"] ~ .shell-sidebar[data-side="end"] { grid-column: 5; --shell-slot-width: var(--shell-end-2-width); }
/* A nested shell takes the main cell of its parent. The shared tokens are inherited explicitly,
   because the recipe emits each default as a class on every shell; the four slot widths are not,
   so an outer panel never sizes an inner drawer. */
.shell > .shell {
  grid-column: main-start / main-end; grid-row: main-start / main-end; min-block-size: 0;
  --shell-sidebar-width: inherit; --shell-header-height: inherit;
  --shell-duration: inherit; --shell-motion: inherit; --shell-ease: inherit;
  --shell-main-max-width: inherit; --shell-gutter: inherit;
}
@media print {
  .shell { block-size: auto; min-block-size: 0; overflow: visible; }
  .shell-sidebar[data-overlay] { display: none; }
}
```

Placement rules:

- Parts place themselves by kind and side, not by DOM order. A sidebar written after main still renders at the start when it says `$side="start"`, so DOM order is free for reading order. Among sidebars on the same side, DOM order is the visual order in the inline direction: the first start sidebar sits at the edge (slot `start-1`), the first end sidebar sits next to main (slot `end-1`).
- Two sidebars per side (D14). A third on one side goes in a nested shell.
- Parts must be direct children of the shell. A wrapper element becomes the grid item and hides a sidebar from its slot; the React root warns in development when a direct child is not a shell part.
- An outer (full-height) sidebar is expressed by nesting (D4): `<Shell><ShellSidebar/><Shell>…</Shell></Shell>`. The inner shell takes the outer main cell, has its own slots, its own container queries and its own centering, and the outer sidebar never enters the inner centering math. Two header bands (a banner above a toolbar) are two nested shells. A footer that must span under an outer sidebar goes in the outer shell.
- The width tokens (`--shell-sidebar-width`, the four slot widths) are registered `<length>` properties with `inherits: true` and `initial-value: 256px`, so an `em` or spacing-number width is computed once on the shell that declares it. A percentage is not a `<length>`: the registered property rejects it at computed-value time and the slot falls back silently to the `initial-value` of 256px, so the React component warns in development when a width prop is a percentage. The shared default inherits into a nested shell; the four slot widths do not.
- Elements and landmarks (D16): the root and the sidebar render `div`, so the page-level `header` and `footer` keep the banner and contentinfo landmarks. The sidebar takes its element through `render` (`nav` for a primary navigation, `aside` for a complementary panel) and documents `aria-label` for multiple same-role landmarks. In overlay mode the sidebar body is the dialog, so a landmark element wraps the content inside the body.

## Header and footer

The bar is a three-column grid, `1fr auto 1fr`, with `align-items: center`, a `0.5rem` gap, inline padding of `max(var(--shell-gutter), env(safe-area-inset-left))` at the physical left edge and `max(var(--shell-gutter), env(safe-area-inset-right))` at the physical right edge (the insets are physical and have no logical form, so the bars use `padding-left` and `padding-right` here and stay logical everywhere else), and a named container `shell-header` (`shell-footer` for the footer). A bare `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, so each side keeps its content minimum: the center part sits on the bar's middle while both sides fit, and moves over when one side needs more than its half. This is GNOME's "loose centering", and the first prototype's defect (every part with `min-width: 0`, which pinned the center while a side ran out of room) is what the default now avoids. GNOME's own default priority is the opposite of this one (its title shrinks before the button groups, then the sides shrink in a second phase, which CSS grid cannot express); D23 keeps the loose-centering template and not that priority.

Parts (D3): `ShellHeaderStart`, `ShellHeaderCenter`, `ShellHeaderEnd` exist as components and recipes, and `<ShellHeader start={…} center={…} end={…}>` renders them through `createRender`. The footer reuses the same part recipes under footer aliases. Parts are flex cells (`display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; min-inline-size: auto`) placed in columns 1, 2 and 3 with `justify-self` start, center and end.

Who gives way (D23): two booleans on each part.

- `$shrink`: the part may lose width. Rules: `max-inline-size: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; min-inline-size: 0; overflow: clip; overflow-clip-margin: 0.25rem`. The cap to the track is required, or the part keeps its content width and paints over its neighbor (up to 110px measured). Text children truncate with the `truncate` utility; a form control keeps its intrinsic minimum (36px different between engines) unless it gets `min-w-0` or a width. `$shrink` is not a way to hide controls: a clipped control cannot be scrolled back into view by script, and a bar that must drop buttons needs an overflow menu, outside the recipe. `overflow-clip-margin` is absent in WebKit 26.5; the portable form is padding with a matching negative margin.
- `$grow`: a side part fills its track (`justify-self: stretch`); the center part takes a double share, and the bar's template becomes `1fr minmax(auto, 2fr) 1fr`, so the center grows while staying centered as long as both sides fit in a quarter of the bar. The center part also takes `justify-self: stretch`. A `$grow` center beside a very wide side sits further off center than a plain one, because the leftover is split two to one (117px against 30px at 900px with a 400px start part); paired with `$shrink` on both sides it stays exactly centered from 900px down to 200px.
- Priority: the grid grows the `auto` center track to its content before it shares leftover space between the flexible sides, so shrinking sides give way before a shrinking center. Strict centering is `$shrink` on both sides (measured identical, to three decimal places, to writing the bar as `minmax(0, 1fr) auto minmax(0, 1fr)` directly). The center is centered exactly when the two side tracks are equal, so `$shrink` on one side re-centers only when that side is the one over its half. `$grow` plus `$shrink` on the center turns the policy into a fixed one-two-one split in which the title truncates while the sides still have room; document that pair as one to avoid.

`$stackCenter` (opt-in): below 40rem of the bar's content width (43rem of bar width with the default gutter, 687px measured in all three engines), the center part moves to a second row spanning the bar. A stacked bar is taller than its height token, so a consumer sets `$headerHeight` to the stacked height in the same container query. Hiding side labels on narrow bars is a consumer container rule on `shell-header`.

`$blur` (D7): `"sm" | "md" | "lg"` emit Tailwind's `backdrop-blur-sm`, `-md` and `-lg` (8px, 12px, 16px) paired with a translucent surface of 85%, 80% and 75% of the layer color (`color-mix(in oklab, …)`), chosen so that bar text stays above 7:1 over any backdrop in either theme; `true` is an alias of `md`. Each step restores an opaque surface where backdrop filters are unsupported (`@supports`) or reduced transparency is requested, and keeps a real border in forced colors. Tailwind 4.3.3 emits `-webkit-backdrop-filter` itself. Document for consumers: `backdrop-filter` creates a stacking context and a containing block for fixed descendants, and nested backdrop filters do not compose.

The footer is always static (D25): no `$sticky`, no height token, no minimum height; it is as tall as its content. A footer that must stay in view is the locked shell's job (see Scroll model). The footer adds `padding-block-end: env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`.

Surfaces: the root inherits the page layer and paints nothing of its own. Header, footer and sidebars extend `frame` and paint a layer half a step off the canvas, with a real border on the edge facing main (borders rather than shadows, because shadows vanish in forced colors). Main paints nothing. An overlay sidebar body paints the same layer and adds a shadow. A blurred bar replaces the opaque layer background with a translucent `color-mix()` of the same layer, because an opaque `ak-layer` background would hide the blur; the `@supports` and reduced-transparency fallbacks restore the opaque value.

```tsx
<ShellHeader $blur="md"
  start={<><ShellSidebarToggle /><Logo /></>}
  center={<ShellHeaderCenter $grow><Search /></ShellHeaderCenter>}
  end={<ShellHeaderEnd $shrink><ThemeSwitch /><UserMenu /></ShellHeaderEnd>}
/>
```

## Sticky parts and the header height

The header and the sidebars accept `$sticky` (D5): `false` scrolls away with the page; `true` stays in view while its row is in view and keeps its space. The header defaults to sticky (D18), the sidebar to sticky. Nothing flows under a bar (a transparent hero header would need a later `$overlap` boolean, not part of v1). No part uses `position: fixed`: an ancestor with `backdrop-filter` or `transform` captures fixed descendants, and a fixed part must know the size of every part before it.

The header height is a declared token (D6): `$headerHeight` sets `--shell-header-height` (default 3.25rem), the header uses it as `min-block-size`, and the root turns it into `--shell-header-offset` only when the header is sticky, with `:has()`. Sticky sidebar bodies read the offset for their top and their maximum height; anchors inside main read it for `scroll-margin`, so a fragment link lands below the bar. That is not the same as WCAG technique C43: C43 is "Using CSS `scroll-padding` to un-obscure content" and puts `scroll-padding-block-start` on the scroll port, which is what keeps a focused control out from under a sticky header (SC 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured, Level AA). In the page-scroll shell the scroll port is `html`, outside the shell's subtree, so the shell publishes `--shell-header-offset` and the docs give the one-line rule consumers copy onto `html` (`scroll-padding-block-start` equal to the header height); in the locked-shell recipe main is the scroll port and the recipe sets `scroll-padding-block-start` there itself.

```css
.shell:has(> .shell-header[data-sticky]) { --shell-header-offset: var(--shell-header-height); }
.shell-header[data-sticky] { position: sticky; inset-block-start: 0; }
.shell-sidebar[data-sticky] > .shell-sidebar-body {
  position: sticky;
  inset-block-start: var(--shell-header-offset);
  max-block-size: calc(100cqb - var(--shell-header-offset));
}
.shell-main [id] { scroll-margin-block-start: calc(var(--shell-header-offset) + 1rem); }
```

Why no footer height exists: a sticky box may never leave its containing block. The sidebar body's containing block is its column, which ends where the footer row begins, so a static footer of any height pushes the body up on its own (experiment E16: 0px covered in three engines, with no token). The bars themselves can stick because every engine constrains a sticky grid item by the grid container, not by its own grid area; the web platform test `css/css-position/sticky/position-sticky-grid.html` pins that down (passing in Chrome 153, Firefox 155 and Safari 26.6 on wpt.fyi stable runs fetched 2026-09-14; the prototypes were measured in the older Chromium 151, Firefox 152 and WebKit 26.5). A literal reading of CSS Grid says otherwise: a grid item's containing block is its grid area whatever its `position`, which a CSSWG editor restates in the comments of `w3c/csswg-drafts#11196` (closed 2024-11-14 with no spec change). Cite the test, not the spec.

Known limit: a header taller than its token (for example, wrapping parts) breaks the sticky offset by the difference (a 97px header with the 3.25rem token hides 45px of the sidebar body). Consumers change the token in a container query; measuring the header with a ResizeObserver was measured to remove the limit and remains a possible later enhancement (D6, not selected).

Stacking: the header is at `z-index: 4`, sidebars at 2, the footer at 3 (nothing overlaps it), an overlay sidebar column at 5 so that a drawer's backdrop dims the header as well as main, all inside the root's `isolation: isolate` and below Ariakit's overlays at 10. Sticky elements create stacking contexts whatever their z-index, so the ladder is not optional. `isolation: isolate` on the root is what stops a consumer's `z-50` banner from competing with chrome; the legacy Ariakit site's `z-40` header painting over a modal dialog and its dim is the defect it prevents. Sticky bars also avoid the classic jump of fixed headers when a modal locks body scroll and the scrollbar disappears: a sticky bar moves with the body's padding compensation, a fixed one does not. Consequences to document: an Ariakit popover, menu or combobox list rendered in place inside a bar or a sidebar lives in that part's stacking context and can be painted over by a later part, so overlays inside chrome should use `portal`; a portalled element leaves the shell's subtree and no longer inherits the shell's tokens.

## Sidebars

A sidebar is a column that the grid sizes and that animates its width, with two children: a backdrop element, painted only in overlay mode, and a body that keeps the full width, scrolls on its own and sticks below the header. Closing the sidebar transitions the column's width to zero while the body stays glued to the edge next to main, so the panel slides out under the shell's edge; the root clips horizontal overflow. When the motion ends, `visibility: hidden` takes the content out of the tab order and the accessibility tree (WCAG 2.4.11; the bug reported against shadcn-ui/ui#7456 and ant-design/ant-design#52853).

```css
.shell-sidebar {
  grid-row: main-start / main-end;
  display: flex; flex-direction: column;
  inline-size: var(--shell-slot-width);   /* the root's token for this slot */
  box-sizing: border-box;
  overflow: clip;                         /* not hidden: hidden would make the column a scroll container and kill the sticky body */
  z-index: 2;
  transition: inline-size var(--shell-time) var(--shell-ease), visibility 0s linear var(--shell-time);
  container: shell-sidebar / inline-size;
  --shell-overlay: 0;                     /* 1 when CSS puts the column in overlay mode */
}
.shell-sidebar[data-open] { transition-delay: 0s, 0s; }
.shell-sidebar:not([data-open]) { inline-size: 0; visibility: hidden; border-inline-width: 0; }
.shell-sidebar-body {
  inline-size: var(--shell-slot-width);   /* no reflow while the column folds */
  box-sizing: border-box; min-block-size: 0; flex: 1 1 auto;
  overflow-y: auto; overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
.shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] > .shell-sidebar-body { margin-inline-start: auto; } /* glued to main */
.shell-sidebar[data-side="end"] > .shell-sidebar-body { margin-inline-end: auto; }
```

No `interpolate-size` (both ends of the width transition are lengths) and no `@starting-style` (the sidebar is never `display: none`, so a state change transitions and a first render does not). The column is not positioned: the backdrop inside it is positioned against the shell root, and container units inside the column would resolve against the column's own zero width in overlay mode.

State ownership (D8): the React component takes the state, in this order, from a `store` prop; from the nearest `DisclosureProvider` or `DialogProvider` (Ariakit's dialog context also provides the disclosure context); or from its own `open`, `defaultOpen` and `onOpenChange` props backing an internal dialog store. A dialog store is used in every case because the same store drives the Dialog that the body becomes in overlay mode. `ShellSidebarToggle` is an Ariakit Disclosure button with a default accessible name (`aria-expanded`, the id link, focus return). Without any store, a static panel is a `div` with `data-open`, defaulting to open. Two facts from the Ariakit source: a store found in context must not be combined with `defaultOpen` (Ariakit throws on conflicting props in development), and when two toggles share one store only the last one used owns the focus return while the other keeps `aria-expanded="false"`, so a shell with both a header toggle and a rail toggle drives that attribute itself.

Persisted state is the consumer's choice: render `data-open` on the server from a cookie, or stamp a flag on the root from an inline script before first paint. A value restored later in an effect animates by design.

```tsx
// Static: always open, no store.
<ShellSidebar aria-label="Documentation" render={<nav />}>…</ShellSidebar>

// Toggleable: a provider owns the state (DialogProvider and DisclosureProvider both work).
<DialogProvider defaultOpen>
  <ShellHeader start={<ShellSidebarToggle />} />
  <ShellSidebar $overlayBelow="md" aria-label="Main">…</ShellSidebar>
</DialogProvider>

// Two on one side: a rail then a panel. DOM order is the visual order from the edge;
// their widths are declared on the root in the same order.
<Shell $startWidth={[16, 64]}>
  <ShellSidebar aria-label="Workspaces">…</ShellSidebar>
  <ShellSidebar aria-label="Channels">…</ShellSidebar>
  …
</Shell>
```

Overlay mode (D9): `$overlay` puts a sidebar in overlay mode at every width; `$overlayBelow: "none" | "sm" | "md" | "lg"` does it below a named step of the shell's width through a container query on the `shell` container, never a media query, because an outer sidebar can make the inner shell narrow without the window changing. Container query sizes cannot use custom properties, so the steps emit fixed rules. The steps follow Tailwind's breakpoint scale applied to the shell container: `md` is 48rem (the default, and the removed component's 768px); `sm` and `lg` take the neighboring steps of the same scale (40rem and 64rem); only `md` was exercised by the experiments. `"none"` keeps the sidebar inline at every width, and different sidebars in one shell can use different steps.

In overlay mode the column reserves nothing and stays visible so the body can slide inside it; the body floats over main with sticky positioning inside the zero-width column; the backdrop covers the shell. `$overlay` emits the rules unconditionally; `$overlayBelow` emits them inside `@container shell (width < <step>)`. The same chains tell main that the slot takes no space.

```css
.shell-sidebar-backdrop {
  display: none; position: absolute; inset: 0;   /* against the root: covers the whole shell */
  background: rgb(0 0 0 / 0.45); opacity: 0; visibility: hidden;
  transition: opacity var(--shell-time) var(--shell-ease), visibility 0s linear var(--shell-time);
}
.shell-sidebar[data-overlay] {
  --shell-overlay: 1; z-index: 5; inline-size: 0; overflow: visible;
  border-inline-width: 0; background: transparent;
  visibility: visible;                    /* overrides the closed column, or the drawer never shows */
}
.shell-sidebar[data-overlay] > .shell-sidebar-backdrop { display: block; }
.shell-sidebar[data-overlay] > .shell-sidebar-body {
  position: sticky;
  inset-block-start: var(--shell-header-offset);
  max-block-size: calc(100cqb - var(--shell-header-offset));
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--shell-edge), 0 24px 48px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5);
  transition: translate var(--shell-time) var(--shell-ease), visibility 0s linear var(--shell-time);
}
.shell-sidebar[data-side="end"][data-overlay] > .shell-sidebar-body { margin-inline-start: calc(-1 * var(--shell-slot-width)); }
.shell-sidebar[data-overlay]:not([data-open]) > .shell-sidebar-body { translate: -100% 0; visibility: hidden; }
/* the end side translates +100%; :dir(rtl) mirrors both signs */
.shell-sidebar[data-open][data-overlay] > .shell-sidebar-backdrop { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; transition-delay: 0s, 0s; }
.shell-sidebar[data-open][data-overlay] > .shell-sidebar-body { transition-delay: 0s, 0s; }
```

The Dialog swap (D10, D24): the body is one `ak.Dialog` element in both modes, never remounted, with `portal={false}` in both. Wide mode: `modal={false}`, `role="none"`, `alwaysVisible`, no backdrop, no focus handling (a `div` with `data-open`). Overlay mode, once hydrated: `modal`, `role="dialog"`, `backdrop={<ShellSidebarBackdrop />}` (Ariakit's `backdrop` prop, which marks the element so a click on it closes the dialog; a plain sibling or a `data-backdrop` sibling does not work), focus in and out, Escape and outside click. Ariakit writes `position: fixed` on that backdrop inline, and also copies the dialog's computed `z-index` onto it inline (`dialog-backdrop.tsx`); the component overrides the position with `absolute` so the backdrop covers the shell rather than the window, and the backdrop's stacking follows the body's z-index rather than the `shellSidebarBackdrop` recipe. What keeps the dim below the panel is DOM order inside the column (the backdrop is the body's previous sibling), and what puts it above the bars is the column's `z-index: 5`; the recipe sets no z-index on the backdrop that the swap could contradict. Three traps, all avoided by that shape: `alwaysVisible` on a modal Dialog locks body scroll forever, so it is a wide-mode prop only; Ariakit detects the leave animation from the computed transition duration, so the body's transition is declared unconditionally and only the animated values change with state; a modal dialog makes the header toggle inert while the drawer is open, so Escape, the backdrop and a close button inside the panel are the ways out, and focus returns to the toggle. `modal={false}` on the sidebar keeps the wide-mode props in overlay mode for consumers who want a non-modal panel.

Overlay detection (D24): the component carries no second breakpoint. It reads the column's `--shell-overlay` flag (a registered `<number>`, `inherits: false`, never transitioned) from computed style in a layout effect on mount, and again whenever a ResizeObserver on the shell root or on the column fires (observing the column catches a root font-size change). Measured: the flag is fresh inside the first callback in all three engines. Before hydration the CSS overlay renders (the panel slides, the backdrop shows) but nothing is inert; modality arrives with JavaScript.

## Main

Main is a named-lines grid (`[full-start] gutter [content-start] content [content-end] gutter [full-end]`) and a query container named `shell-main`, with `min-inline-size: 0` and `padding-block: var(--shell-gutter)`. Children land in the content column; a child with `grid-column: full` spans the gutters (`ShellBleed`, or `$bleed` on `Frame`), with `contain: inline-size` so a wide table or an embedded shell does not stretch the auto gutters. Every direct child of main is a grid item, so grid items do not collapse margins; the docs recommend `Prose` as the text container.

Variants (D12): `$centered: boolean | "main"`, `$maxWidth` (default 48rem), `$gutter` (default 1.5rem, the minimum side space, also main's block padding). Full width is the default. `$centered` centers the content column on the shell; `$centered="main"` centers it within main only, with no compensation.

Centering that accounts for the sidebars (D11, D22): each slot's space is its declared width times an open flag (1 when that slot's sidebar is open and takes space; 0 when closed, absent or an overlay). The flags come from `:has()` chains on the root; the spaces are registered `<length>` properties transitioned on the shell's shared duration and easing (`var(--shell-time) var(--shell-ease)`), so main's compensation moves in step with the drawer that is folding. The difference between the sides becomes the minimum of the gutter on the emptier side, clamped to the free space beyond the content's maximum width; the gutters are `auto` tracks, which the grid stretches by equal amounts, so the offset between them survives (`1fr` gutters would collapse it).

```css
@property --shell-start-1-space { syntax: "<length>"; inherits: false; initial-value: 0px; }
/* one per slot: start-2, end-1, end-2 */
.shell:where(:has(> .shell-sidebar[data-side="start"][data-open]:not(.shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] ~ *))) > .shell-main { --shell-start-1-open: 1; }
.shell:where(:has(> .shell-sidebar[data-side="start"] ~ .shell-sidebar[data-side="start"][data-open])) > .shell-main { --shell-start-2-open: 1; }
/* end-1, end-2 alike; the same chains, outside :where(), force a slot to 0 when its sidebar is an overlay */
.shell-main {
  grid-column: main-start / main-end; grid-row: main-start / main-end;
  container: shell-main / inline-size;
  display: grid; align-content: start; justify-content: stretch;
  padding-block: var(--shell-gutter); min-inline-size: 0;
  grid-template-columns:
    [full-start] var(--shell-gutter)
    [content-start] minmax(0, 1fr) [content-end]
    var(--shell-gutter) [full-end];      /* the default, full-width template */
  --shell-start-1-open: 0; --shell-start-2-open: 0; --shell-end-1-open: 0; --shell-end-2-open: 0;
  --shell-start-1-space: calc(var(--shell-start-1-width) * var(--shell-start-1-open)); /* ×4 */
  transition: --shell-start-1-space var(--shell-time) var(--shell-ease) /* , ×4 */;
  --shell-start-total: calc(max(0px, var(--shell-start-1-space)) + max(0px, var(--shell-start-2-space)));
  --shell-end-total:   calc(max(0px, var(--shell-end-1-space)) + max(0px, var(--shell-end-2-space)));
  --shell-comp-end:    max(0px, calc(var(--shell-start-total) - var(--shell-end-total)));
  --shell-comp-start:  max(0px, calc(var(--shell-end-total) - var(--shell-start-total)));
}
.shell-main[data-centered] {
  grid-template-columns:
    [full-start] minmax(clamp(var(--shell-gutter), calc(100% - var(--shell-main-max-width) - var(--shell-gutter)), calc(var(--shell-gutter) + var(--shell-comp-start))), auto)
    [content-start] minmax(0, var(--shell-main-max-width)) [content-end]
    minmax(clamp(var(--shell-gutter), calc(100% - var(--shell-main-max-width) - var(--shell-gutter)), calc(var(--shell-gutter) + var(--shell-comp-end))), auto) [full-end];
}
.shell-main[data-centered="main"] { --shell-comp-end: 0px; --shell-comp-start: 0px; }
.shell-main > * { grid-column: content; min-inline-size: 0; }
.shell-main > .shell-bleed { grid-column: full; contain: inline-size; }
```

Seven invariants keep the model exact and are part of the contract. Invariants 1 to 4, 6 and the per-part-duration half of 7 carry numbers from experiment E13 and its adversarial re-check; invariant 5 and the reduced-motion half of 7 follow from the registration syntax and the stylesheet and are test obligations, not measurements:

1. The four slot flags are declared as 0 on main, so a nested shell's main never inherits an outer shell's flags (128px off without them).
2. Each slot space is clamped at zero when the sides are summed, because an overshooting easing can take a registered length below zero while the column stops at zero (24px error without the clamp, 0.5px worst with it).
3. The flag setters sit in `:where()` and the overlay overrides repeat the full `:has()` chain, so the overrides win by specificity regardless of source order.
4. The width tokens are registered lengths, so an `em` or spacing-number width is computed once on the shell that declares it (32px error unregistered).
5. A percentage width is not a `<length>`: the registration makes it invalid at computed-value time, so the slot silently falls back to the shared default (256px initially) rather than throwing. The recipe rejects it, and a development warning surfaces it.
6. The four slot widths are per shell and do not inherit into a nested shell; the shared default width, the header height, the duration, the motion multiplier, the easing, the main max width and the gutter do, through explicit `inherit` declarations on `.shell > .shell`, because the recipe re-declares every default on every shell.
7. Every shell motion runs on the root's duration and easing through one multiplier (`--shell-motion`) that `prefers-reduced-motion` zeroes in the stylesheet, so an inline `--shell-duration` from `$duration` cannot defeat reduced motion, and a per-part duration utility (which would desynchronize the drawer and the compensation by up to 64px) is not supported. `transition-none` on the root must disable the drawer and the compensation together.

What gives way when there is no room (D21, reading width first): full centering needs the shell to be at least as wide as the open sidebars plus the content maximum plus two gutters plus the difference between the sides (1328px with the defaults and one 16rem sidebar). Below that, the compensation shrinks first and the content column keeps its width, drifting toward the sidebar by up to half the sidebar's width while the header's center part stays on the page center; the content width shrinks last. Document the threshold formula.

```tsx
// Docs layout: nav 16rem at the start, table of contents 12rem at the end (spacing numbers).
<Shell $startWidth={64} $endWidth={48} $headerHeight={13}>
  <ShellHeader … />
  <ShellSidebar aria-label="Documentation">…</ShellSidebar>
  <ShellMain $centered $maxWidth={256} $gutter={6}>
    <Prose>…</Prose>
    <ShellBleed>A full-width band inside the centered main</ShellBleed>
    <Prose>…</Prose>
  </ShellMain>
  <ShellSidebar $side="end" aria-label="On this page">…</ShellSidebar>
</Shell>
```

## Scroll model

The shell scrolls with the page, and only with the page (D13): anchors, find-in-page and scroll restoration behave, and the print rule above releases the root's minimum height and clipping and hides overlay sidebars. A locked, main-scrolling shell is a consumer recipe on top of the same grid, because the bar rows are `auto` and the main row is `minmax(0, 1fr)`:

```tsx
<Shell className="h-dvh [container-type:size]">
  <ShellHeader … />
  <ShellSidebar … />
  <ShellMain className="overflow-auto overscroll-contain [&_[id]]:scroll-mt-0">…</ShellMain>
  <ShellFooter … />
</Shell>
```

Facts the recipe must document (measured in three engines): the document never grows and hash navigation scrolls main; main is the scroller, so the shell's `scroll-margin` on anchors (a 68px error there) is switched off; the sidebar body's height cap uses container units that fall back to the small viewport, so the locked root declares itself a size container; the recipe assumes a zero body margin (Tailwind preflight). This is also how a footer stays in view: the footer row sizes itself from its content, with no token (E16, 0px covered by a four-line footer). A modal Dialog in a locked shell needs nothing extra: its inert marking already stops wheel scrolling of main in all three engines, and `preventBodyScroll={false}` only removes a useless body mutation and the iOS fixed-body path. A non-modal dialog leaves main scrollable, and naming an inner scroller for Ariakit's scroll lock is a separate API decision.

## Phones and tablets

Under the breakpoint, sidebars marked for overlay become modal drawers with a backdrop, slide from their edge, trap focus and make the rest of the shell inert once JavaScript has run. The header's center part truncates, moves over or stacks. The footer stays a static bar. The bars already leave room for safe areas: their physical left and right padding is `max(var(--shell-gutter), env(safe-area-inset-left))` and `max(var(--shell-gutter), env(safe-area-inset-right))`, and the footer adds `padding-block-end: env(safe-area-inset-bottom)`, so an inset larger than the gutter simply widens the padding (`viewport-fit=cover` is the consumer's meta tag). Height units are container units that fall back to the small viewport, the conservative choice for phone browser toolbars. A Dialog also brings the virtual-keyboard height Ariakit measures into `--dialog-viewport-height`. Real iOS Safari and Android Chrome behavior is not yet verified (see Test obligations).

## The contract

### Recipes (`packages/ariakit-ui/src/styles/shell.ts`)

| Recipe | Extends | Variants | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `shell` | `layer` | `$sidebarWidth`, `$startWidth`, `$endWidth` (a width or a pair, D22), `$headerHeight`, `$duration` | Grid, container `shell`, slot tokens and the header offset. Numbers scale the spacing token, as elsewhere in Ariakit UI. |
| `shellHeader` | `frame` | `$sticky` (default true, D5 and D18), `$blur: true \| "sm" \| "md" \| "lg"`, `$stackCenter` | Three-column grid `1fr auto 1fr`, container `shell-header`, `min-block-size` from the token. |
| `shellHeaderStart`, `shellHeaderCenter`, `shellHeaderEnd` | none | `$shrink`, `$grow` (D23) | Flex cells that keep their content minimum by default. Footer parts alias them. |
| `shellSidebar` | `frame` | `$side: "start" \| "end"`, `$sticky` (default true), `$overlay`, `$overlayBelow: "none" \| "sm" \| "md" \| "lg"` | The animated column; width from its slot token; container `shell-sidebar`; the `--shell-overlay` flag. |
| `shellSidebarBackdrop` | none | none | Painted in overlay mode only; a previous sibling of the body. |
| `shellSidebarBody` | `frameBase` | `$p` | Fixed width from the slot token, scrolls, sticks below the header; the element that becomes the Dialog. |
| `shellMain` | `frameBase` | `$centered: boolean \| "main"`, `$maxWidth`, `$gutter` | Named-lines grid, container `shell-main`, slot flags and transitioned slot spaces, `scroll-margin` on anchors. |
| `shellBleed` | none | none | `grid-column: full; contain: inline-size`. Also available as `$bleed` on `Frame`. |
| `shellFooter` | `frame` | `$blur` | Same grid as the header, bottom row. Always static and content-sized (D25); no height token. |

Emission rules (clava 0.6.3): a function variant takes a pair or a boolean-or-string union as its typed parameter, so `$startWidth={[16, 64]}` and `$blur: true | "sm" | "md" | "lg"` need no second variant (`$border` on `Frame` is the precedent for `true` meaning the default step). Numbers scale the spacing token, which is em-based: `$sidebarWidth={64}` is 16em of the shell's own font, so a shell that must not follow its font size passes a rem string. Token defaults and their fallback chains are emitted as classes, so a container rule can move them; caller values go to the style attribute, where they outrank the defaults; state flags and the `:has()` chains are classes only. The `@property` registrations (the four slot spaces, `--shell-overlay`, the five width tokens) live in `ui.css` beside the existing ones. Recipes never emit `data-*` attributes: `data-side`, `data-open`, `data-sticky`, `data-overlay`, `data-overlay-below`, `data-blur`, `data-shrink`, `data-grow`, `data-stack-center` and `data-centered` come from the React components, and the recipes supply the selectors. Rendering variants: `ui-open:` and `ui-closed:` cover Ariakit's `data-open`, so the sidebar body works as a plain div, a `DisclosureContent` or a `Dialog`.

### React components (`shell.ariakit.react.tsx`)

- `Shell` renders a `div` and emits the slot tokens. `ShellHeader` renders a `header` with `start`, `center` and `end` render props. `ShellMain` renders a `main`. `ShellBleed` a `div`. `ShellFooter` a `footer`, always static.
- `ShellSidebar` renders the column and, inside it, one `ak.Dialog` as the body, with `portal={false}` in both modes. State comes from a `store` prop, the nearest `DisclosureProvider` or `DialogProvider`, or its own `open`, `defaultOpen` and `onOpenChange` props. Wide mode: `modal={false}`, `role="none"`, `alwaysVisible`, no backdrop, no focus handling. Overlay mode, once hydrated: `modal`, `role="dialog"`, `backdrop={<ShellSidebarBackdrop />}`, focus in and out, Escape and outside click. `modal={false}` on the sidebar keeps wide-mode props in overlay mode. A `body` render prop customizes the body element (D15).
- `ShellSidebarToggle` renders an Ariakit Disclosure button with a default accessible name.
- One ResizeObserver per sidebar, on the shell root and on the column, to re-read the overlay flag; the mount value is read in a layout effect. No breakpoint in JavaScript, no measurement of sizes.
- Development warnings: a direct child of the shell that is not a shell part; a sidebar column whose rendered width differs from its slot token; a width prop given as a percentage; `$sticky` passed to `ShellFooter`.

### Custom properties

| Property | Set by | Read by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--shell-sidebar-width`, `--shell-start-1-width`, `--shell-start-2-width`, `--shell-end-1-width`, `--shell-end-2-width` (registered lengths) | root defaults; `$sidebarWidth`, `$startWidth`, `$endWidth` | each sidebar column and body (as `--shell-slot-width`), main's slot spaces |
| `--shell-header-height` | root default; `$headerHeight` | the header (`min-block-size`), the root (offset) |
| `--shell-header-offset` | root, from `:has()` on a sticky header | sticky sidebar bodies, anchors in main |
| `--shell-start-1-open` … `--shell-end-2-open` | main, from `:has()` chains and overlay rules | main's slot spaces |
| `--shell-start-1-space` … `--shell-end-2-space` (registered lengths) | main, transitioned | main's compensation |
| `--shell-overlay` (registered number) | the sidebar column, from `$overlay` or the breakpoint rule | the React component (Dialog swap) |
| `--shell-main-max-width`, `--shell-gutter` | root defaults; main's `$maxWidth` and `$gutter` | main's grid; the bars read `--shell-gutter` for their inline padding, which is why 40rem of `$stackCenter` content width is 43rem of bar width |
| `--shell-duration`, `--shell-motion`, `--shell-time`, `--shell-ease` | root; `--shell-motion` is 0 under reduced motion | every transition in the shell |

### Observable behavior, in short

- Parts place themselves by kind and side; DOM order is free between kinds and is the visual order among same-side sidebars.
- A sticky header or sidebar keeps its space and its place; a static part scrolls away. Nothing flows under a bar. The footer is always static.
- A bar's center part sits on the bar's middle while both sides fit, moves over when one side needs the room, and gives way only where `$shrink` says so; sides give way before the center.
- A closed sidebar takes no space, is invisible to keyboard and assistive technology once its motion ends, and reopens without a first-paint animation. In overlay mode with JavaScript it is a modal dialog.
- A centered main sits on the shell's axis for any mix of open, closed or uneven sidebars, follows every drawer motion, and gives the compensation up before it gives up width.
- Everything mirrors in RTL; everything except modality works with JavaScript disabled.

## Decision record

All 25 decisions are settled by the maintainer. The design document keeps the full rationale, the rejected options and the evidence for each.

| ID | Question | Decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D1 | Root component name | `Shell` (persistent chrome around changing content; no collision with Frame, Layer or HTML names). |
| D2 | Part names | `ShellHeader`, `ShellSidebar`, `ShellMain`, `ShellFooter`. |
| D3 | Bar parts | Part components plus `start`, `center` and `end` render props on the bar. |
| D4 | Full-height (outer) sidebar | Nesting: a shell inside a shell takes the outer main cell. |
| D5 | Staying in view | A `$sticky` boolean on the header and the sidebar; no `fixed` mode; the footer has none (D25). |
| D6 | Header height | Declared token `$headerHeight`, applied as the header's minimum height; measurement is a possible later enhancement. |
| D7 | Header blur | `$blur: "sm" \| "md" \| "lg"` (`true` = `md`), each step bundling radius, translucency and fallbacks. |
| D8 | Sidebar open state | `open` prop, plus a `store` prop or the nearest Disclosure or Dialog provider; `ShellSidebarToggle` gets the button semantics. |
| D9 | Overlay breakpoint | `$overlayBelow` named steps, default `"md"` (48rem of shell width), as a container query on the shell. |
| D10 | Modality on narrow shells | The body swaps to a modal Ariakit Dialog in overlay mode. |
| D11 | Centered main and sidebars | Declared slot widths times open flags, transitioned slot spaces, the difference spent as the emptier gutter. |
| D12 | Main variants | `$centered`, `$maxWidth`, `$gutter`; no `$balance`. |
| D13 | Scroll model | Page scroll only; the locked shell is a consumer recipe. |
| D14 | Sidebars per side | Two per side, placed by sibling selectors. |
| D15 | Sidebar body | Auto-rendered `ShellSidebarBody` with a `body` render prop. A sidebar's own header and footer rows compose inside the body as a flex column; named parts for them are a follow-up. |
| D16 | Default elements | `div` root and sidebar; `header`, `main`, `footer` bars; `render` for `nav` or `aside`. |
| D17 | Collapsed icon rail | Follow-up after v1, designed with `Nav`. |
| D18 | Header sticky by default | Yes. |
| D19 | Non-goals | Resizable sidebars, hide-on-scroll header, split panes, docked tab panels and swipe gestures are confirmed non-goals. |
| D20 | Standalone bar, drawer and column recipes | Shell parts only in v1. |
| D21 | When compensation does not fit | Reading width first: the content column keeps its maximum width and the compensation gives way. No measurement is involved; the policy is the `clamp()` in main's gutter tracks. |
| D22 | Where widths live | On the root: `$sidebarWidth`, `$startWidth`, `$endWidth`, each a width or a pair. |
| D23 | Bar part priority | `$shrink` and `$grow` booleans on each part. |
| D24 | Overlay detection for the Dialog swap | Read the computed `--shell-overlay` flag on mount and on resize. |
| D25 | Footer height | No sticky footer and no footer token; footers are always static; a pinned footer is a locked-shell layout. |

### Rejected alternatives

- No component (Frame plus Tailwind grid classes per app): consumers rewrite the offset, sticky, overlay and centering rules and get them wrong.
- Declared sizes with `position: fixed` parts (Mantine, Atlassian): captured by transformed or blurred ancestors, sizes duplicated across parts, iOS scroll lock issues.
- Measured space in CSS (`100cqi - 100%`): cannot tell two sidebars apart, so two-sided layouts center between the sidebars and need a hand-computed `$balance`.
- Measured layout with a ResizeObserver for widths and heights: first paint without measurements; kept as an enhancement path only.
- Open-aware flags on measured space, immediate or delayed: a jump at one end of the animation with two sidebars, up to 118px (E3).
- Subgrid sidebars for token-free sticky offsets: a subgrid cannot cap its own height, so it never sticks (E2).
- Anchor positioning for the bar offsets: `anchor()` and `anchor-size()` resolve only on absolutely positioned boxes, and nothing inside the sticky body can anchor to a bar (containing-block rule; measured).
- A footer height token, a sticky footer in the page-scroll shell, and a measured footer: removed by D25.
- `fr` gutter tracks for compensation: the offset collapses when free space exists (E3).
- One transitioned difference instead of per-slot spaces: a transient of up to 32.8px during overlapping drawer motions (E13).
- `min-width: 0` on every bar part: turns loose centering into strict centering (the r1 defect).
- `interpolate-size` for the drawer: unnecessary and Chromium-only.
- A CSS-only modal drawer: impossible (inertness needs JavaScript or a dialog).
- A JavaScript breakpoint (`matchMedia`) for the Dialog swap: can disagree with the container query.
- A plain sibling backdrop, or one carrying `data-backdrop`, in Dialog mode: the first closes the dialog only by accident, the second never (E15).
- Swapping component types at the breakpoint or rendering the navigation twice: one `ak.Dialog` with flipping props never remounts (E15).

## Risks and known limits

- The header token drifts from reality when the header wraps (45px hidden with a 97px header). Mitigation: `min-block-size` on the header, a documented token, container-query overrides.
- A consumer expects a sticky footer. Mitigation: docs, the locked shell recipe, a development warning if `$sticky` is passed to `ShellFooter`.
- Centering below the width threshold (about 1328px with the defaults): the column drifts toward the sidebar and moves when sidebars toggle. Mitigation: the threshold formula in the docs.
- Slot re-assignment: mounting a same-side sidebar ahead of an existing one re-slots both and animates the existing drawer's width; appending after it does not. Mitigation: keep DOM order stable or keep both mounted and toggle `open`.
- Firefox animates the overlay flip on first paint on the container-query path when the page loads narrow with an open sidebar (a 58px jump); the `$overlay` path is exact everywhere. Mitigation: documented; a persisted closed state on narrow shells.
- The CSS `zoom` property: Firefox applies it twice to a registered length and WebKit divides a rem token by it; browser zoom is unaffected. Mitigation: unsupported inside a zoomed ancestor.
- A sidebar wider than its declared slot breaks the centering by the difference. Mitigation: no width variant on the sidebar; a development warning.
- Pre-hydration overlay is not modal. Mitigation: documented; persisted closed state on phones in the examples.
- In-place modal Dialog behaviors were read from the Ariakit source and run in a Vite probe, not yet in a repository sandbox.
- Popovers inside the shell do not portal by default; a scrolling sidebar body clips them and a blurred header captures them. Mitigation: docs, `portal` in the Nav examples.
- Grid-item children in main change margin collapsing. Mitigation: docs, `Prose` as the text container.
- Focus rings near a sidebar edge or in a `$shrink` part can be clipped by `overflow: clip`. Mitigation: body padding, a clip margin (padding plus negative margin where `overflow-clip-margin` is absent), a visual test.
- Form controls in a shrinking part keep their intrinsic minimum. Mitigation: docs; the search example sets a width.
- Nested backdrop filters do not compose. Mitigation: docs.
- Wrappers around parts break placement and slot detection. Mitigation: docs and a development warning.
- Window resizes across the overlay breakpoint animate the width and the compensation. Acceptable in v1; a resizing flag is a documented enhancement.
- Firefox 152 did not re-evaluate a rem-based size container query after a root font-size change when the query's rule also changes layout inside the container. Mitigation: the steps are the same in px; an upstream report belongs in the test plan.
- Safe areas cannot be tested in the preview harness. Mitigation: the `env()` terms are already in the bars' padding, `viewport-fit=cover` is a documented consumer requirement, and a real-device check is in the test plan.

## Compatibility

New public surface, plus a `$bleed` variant added to the existing `Frame` recipe; no existing behavior changes. Container query units, `:has()`, `overflow: clip`, container style queries, `color-mix()` and registered custom properties are Baseline in 2026; the repository already relies on the first four, and Tailwind 4 registers its own properties with `@property`. No `interpolate-size`, no anchor positioning, no subgrid.

## Test obligations

- Sandbox `app/src/sandbox/ariakit-ui-shell/` with one example per use case above, plus regression fixtures at the end, per the repository's sandbox rules.
- Browser tests (Playwright, three engines): geometry of every sticky combination at two widths and in RTL; the content column's offset from the shell center with zero, one and two sidebars, uneven widths, each one closed, at three widths, and sampled during a drawer motion; sticky body bounds below a sticky header, including a static footer of any height pushing the body up at the end of the page, and the locked shell with a four-line footer; anchor landing under a sticky header; drawer open and close with keyboard reachability before and after; header part priority for the default, shrink and grow cases; overlay mode from a container width change; the Dialog swap (role, focus in and out, inert siblings, Escape, backdrop click, body scroll lock, no swap with `modal={false}`, a root font-size change flipping the mode); the centering threshold at the shipped defaults; reduced motion with an inline `$duration`; nested shell container query and slot widths per shell.
- Visual captures in light and dark, and under forced colors and reduced transparency for the blurred header at each step.
- Accessibility checks: landmark tree (one banner, one main, one contentinfo, labelled navigations); tab order with a closed sidebar; a tab-through of a page with a sticky header, asserting that every focused control's rectangle is fully below the header (SC 2.4.11) with the documented `scroll-padding` rule in place; focus return to the toggle after the dialog closes.
- Recipe checks: emitted class and style comparison for every variant, including the slot tokens from a single width and from a pair; a probe that `transition-none` disables the drawer and the compensation together.
- Static acceptance test: the shell renders correctly, with a persisted open state, from an Astro layout with zero bytes of component JavaScript, and the overlay drawer still opens and closes from a plain toggle.
- Real devices: iOS Safari and Android Chrome for overlay mode, sticky bars, the in-place modal dialog and the locked-shell recipe, through the BrowserStack workflow.

## Evidence

Sixteen experiments (E1 to E16) drove standalone prototypes with Playwright in Chromium 151, Firefox 152 and WebKit 26.5 on headless macOS; every layout claim above is identical in the three engines unless a risk item says otherwise. The runs were headless on macOS, with no real iOS or Android device and no screen reader; reduced motion and forced colors were not emulated and belong in the test plan; the Dialog swap was exercised in a Vite probe with the real Ariakit source (E15), not through the repository. Three Opus 5 workflows produced 37 research reports: 21 on prior art (Tailwind, React, Vue and enterprise systems, docs frameworks, platform models), CSS mechanics, standards, issue mining and naming; 9 that re-checked the r2 proposals adversarially; 7 on the footer question that settled D25. The prototypes, the JSON measurements and the reports are published with the design document linked at the top.

| Experiment | What it settled |
| --- | --- |
| E1 | Three-part bar: with parts at their content minimum the center offset is 0 while both sides fit, then moves over (16px with a 300px side in a 284px half); `minmax(0, 1fr)` sides overlap the center instead; a stacked center lands on row 2 below the threshold. |
| E2 | A subgridded sidebar is stretched to the rows it spans, so its height cap is ignored (2006px box against a 100dvh cap; body top at -449px after a 500px scroll) and it never sticks. |
| E3 | `fr` gutter tracks collapse the compensation whenever free space exists (24px error); open-aware flags over measured space jump by up to 118px at one end of the animation with two sidebars. |
| E4, E5 | A fixed child of an inline-size container is not captured; a fixed child of a blurred or filtered box is. |
| E6, E7 | Overlay without `position: fixed`: a zero-width sticky column with the body floating over main and a backdrop covering the shell; the closed body is translated away, hidden and unfocusable; the drawer folds to 0 with main widening in step and no horizontal scrollbar. |
| E8 | A sticky child of an `overflow: clip` box still sticks; under `overflow: hidden` it does not. |
| E9 | The locked shell: the document height stays at the viewport while the inner shell scrolls; the sticky header and the nav body keep their offsets. |
| E10 | Nested shells: the inner main compensates only the inner slots; the inner container query fires when the outer rail opens without the window changing. |
| E11, E12 | The sidebar body scrolls as a flex child of the column, not as a grid child; RTL mirrors every measurement; an anchor lands under the header with `scroll-margin`. |
| E13 | Centering from declared widths: 0px offset from the shell center at rest for every open, closed and uneven combination, LTR and RTL, and at every 30ms sample through single, simultaneous and overlapping drawer motions; +32px in the partial regime at 800px; one transitioned difference instead of four slot spaces gave up to 32.8px of transient; the overshoot clamp took a spring easing from 24px to 0.5px; registered width tokens removed a 32px `em` error. |
| E14 | Header part priority from 900px to 400px: default parts keep their minimum and the center moves over (+49px at 560px), overflowing only when nothing fits; `$shrink` on the start keeps the offset at 0 while the start track falls to 93px; all three shrinking keeps the center while the sides shrink first; `$grow` on the center gives a 410px center track at 900px. |
| E15 | Ariakit Dialog with `modal` and `portal={false}` in place: siblings inert, ancestors untouched, focus returns to the toggle on Escape; only the Dialog's `backdrop` prop closes on click; `alwaysVisible` on a modal Dialog locks body scroll before it opens; a transition declared only under `[data-open]` gives a 0ms leave; the overlay flag is fresh inside the first ResizeObserver callback (18 of 18 flips); the locked shell's anchor `scroll-margin` overshoots by 68px and inert main stops wheel scrolling. |
| E16 | A static footer of any height never covers the sticky sidebar body (0px) and pushes it up at the end of the page with no footer token; a sticky four-line footer at a 3rem token covered 53px, which is why sticky footers were removed; a 97px header against the 3.25rem token hides 45px of the body; a locked shell with a four-line footer needs no token. |

External references that shaped the contract (third-party issues are written as plain text on purpose, so that this issue does not post cross-reference notifications on them): CSS Positioned Layout 3 (sticky positioning); CSS Grid (track sizing order and flexible tracks); CSS Overflow 3 (`overflow: clip` does not create a scroll container, so sticky keeps working under it); CSS Properties and Values API (registered custom properties and their transitions); the web platform test `css/css-position/sticky/position-sticky-grid.html` and `w3c/csswg-drafts#11196`; CSS Anchor Positioning 1 (resolution rules); the HTML "update the rendering" steps and Resize Observer 1; HTML-AAM landmark mapping; WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) and technique C43, "Using CSS `scroll-padding` to un-obscure content"; the 2024 CSSWG resolution on `w3c/csswg-drafts#10544` (`container-type` does not force layout containment, only an independent formatting context, so it no longer establishes a containing block for absolutely and fixed positioned descendants); `ant-design/ant-design#52411` (the Ant Design docs moved from fixed to sticky siders); `shadcn-ui/ui#7456` and `ant-design/ant-design#52853` (collapsed sidebars still in the tab order); `shadcn-ui/ui#8868` and `shadcn-ui/ui#5629` (provider-owned single state blocks two sidebars and a header above the sidebar); `shadcn-ui/ui#5864` and `ant-design/ant-design#8937` (a JavaScript breakpoint disagreeing with the server, and a first-paint flash of the auto-detected sider); `shadcn-ui/ui#11370`, `shadcn-ui/ui#10549` and `shadcn-ui/ui#7853` (main without `min-width: 0`, the three open reports behind main's `minmax(0, 1fr)` track and `min-inline-size: 0`); `vuetifyjs/vuetify#11121` (the declared footer-height bug that pushed Vuetify 3 to measuring); `vuetifyjs/vuetify#20065` and `quasarframework/quasar#18276` (the ResizeObserver loop errors that measuring costs, both closed as not planned); `facebook/docusaurus#8789` (a declared bar height that cuts its own content).

Unresolved claims that the tests above must settle: real iOS Safari behavior for sticky overlay bodies, the in-place modal's body scroll lock and the locked-shell recipe; screen reader announcement of a `nav` sidebar beside a page-level `nav` and of the dialog role in overlay mode; blur rendering outside Chromium (headless Firefox and WebKit did not rasterize backdrop filters); real browser page zoom; a WebKit report of sticky children jittering inside a one-axis `overflow: clip` container (none seen in WebKit 26.5); the Dialog-mode stacking of the backdrop against the bars.

comments.body:
You're right. Requiring the entire test suite to use a VM pool is too broad.

Updated triage outcome: `ariakit-bug`.

The invalid `Window` value originates in Vitest's non-VM jsdom environment, but `fireClickEvent` can handle it without changing its behavior in real browsers. Because this currently breaks keyboard activation through the stable public `@ariakit/react` API, I am reopening the issue and tracking it as a bug.

A narrower workaround is to restore `document.defaultView` to Vitest's underlying jsdom window in the existing setup file:

```ts
/// <reference types="vitest/jsdom" />

if (typeof jsdom !== "undefined") {
  Object.defineProperty(document, "defaultView", {
    configurable: true,
    value: jsdom.window,
  });
}
```

Vitest [documents the `jsdom` global](https://vitest.dev/config/environment), so this does not require importing jsdom or changing the pool. I verified that it fixes both Enter and Space activation with the default `forks` pool.

The tradeoff is that `document.defaultView` becomes the genuine jsdom `Window`, while Vitest's `window` global remains its Node global context. Therefore, `document.defaultView === window` becomes `false`. This is narrower than moving the whole suite to `vmThreads`, but suites that depend on that Vitest-specific identity should account for it.

The proposed Ariakit fix is also small. Before supplying `UIEventInit.view`, check whether the value is recognized as a `Window` by its own realm:

```diff
-function getClickEventInit(view: Window, eventInit?: PointerEventInit | null) {
+function getClickEventInit(
+  view: Window & typeof globalThis,
+  eventInit?: PointerEventInit | null,
+) {
   const init = eventInit ?? {};
+  const eventView = view instanceof view.Window ? view : null;

   return new Proxy({}, {
     get(_target, key) {
-      if (key === "view") return view;
+      if (key === "view") return eventView;
```

This preserves the owning window in real browsers and same-origin frames. It falls back to `null` only when the event constructor would reject the value and throw.

I tested this candidate on current [`988247dd8`](https://github.com/ariakit/ariakit/commit/988247dd804f26610d354edb9a83b3cb0e772856):

- The jsdom Enter and Space reproduction passes with Vitest's default pool.
- The existing `@ariakit/utils` event tests and targeted TypeScript check pass.
- All 21 existing command browser checks pass across Chrome, Firefox, and WebKit, including the `view`, `composed`, iframe, and shadow-root assertions.

This lets us keep the browser-parity behavior added for #7192 while degrading gracefully in the Vitest environment.
